            },
            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 68/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses detailed BOP cost data to examine operating expenses, budget allocations, and aging inmate impacts; lacks revenue discussion and broader demographic analysis.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "Since the early 1980s, there has been a historically unprecedented increase in the federal prison population. Some of the growth is attributable to changes in federal criminal justice policy during the previous three decades. An issue before Congress is whether policymakers consider the rate of growth in the federal prison population sustainable, and if not, what changes could be made to federal criminal justice policy to reduce the prison population while maintaining public safety. This report explores the issues related to the growing federal prison population.",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "The per capita cost of incarceration for all federal inmates increased from $19,571 in FY2000 to $26,094 in FY2011. Appropriations for the BOP rose from $3.668 billion to $6.381 billion in the same period. The per capita cost of medical care, utilities, and food also increased, with medical costs showing the most pronounced rise. The BOP reported a backlog of 154 modernization and repair projects costing approximately $349 million for FY2012.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "The federal prison population is aging, with approximately 14% of federal inmates over 50 and nearly 4% over 60 by FY2010. Older individuals generally require more medical care, contributing to rising healthcare costs. The BOP reported that an increasing number of federal inmates need medical care, partly due to conditions like diabetes, hypertension, and infectious diseases being more prevalent in the incarcerated population. Granting early release to elderly offenders would have minimal effect on prison overcrowding. Other demographic characteristics like race, gender, or socioeconomic status are not addressed.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "The per capita cost of incarceration for all federal inmates rose from $19,571 in FY2000 to $26,094 in FY2011. Appropriations for the BOP increased from $3.668 billion to $6.381 billion during this period. In FY2012, housing an inmate in a high-security facility cost approximately $34,000, while a minimum-security facility cost nearly $22,000. Per-inmate costs for utilities, food, and medical care increased since FY2000.",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "The per capita cost of incarceration for all federal inmates increased from $19,571 in FY2000 to $26,094 in FY2011. In FY2012, it cost approximately $34,000 to house an inmate in a high-security facility, compared to $27,000 for medium/low-security and $22,000 for minimum-security. The BOP's appropriations increased over $6 billion from FY1980 ($330 million) to FY2012 ($6.641 billion). Per-inmate costs for medical care, utilities, and food have increased since FY2000, with medical costs rising faster than inflation. In FY2012, the BOP spent nearly $275 more per inmate for education programs than in FY2000. No revenue generation is mentioned.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "The per capita cost of federal incarceration rose from $19,571 in FY2000 to $26,094 in FY2011, an increase of 34.4%. In FY2012, housing an inmate in a high-security facility cost approximately $34,000, while a minimum-security facility cost nearly $22,000. Rising utility, food, and medical care costs are primary drivers of increasing system costs. Medical costs are increasing due to an aging inmate population (14% over 50, 4% over 60 in FY2010) and higher incidence of conditions like diabetes and hypertension. State correctional spending has nearly quadrupled in two decades.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "The federal prison population increased from approximately 25,000 in FY1980 to nearly 219,000 in FY2012, growing by about 6,100 inmates annually. Immigration and weapons-related offenses account for a growing proportion of inmates, though drug offenses remain the largest portion. Approximately 7 in 10 inmates are sentenced for five years or less. By FY2010, about 14% of federal inmates were over 50 and nearly 4% over 60 years old."
        },
        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "id": 2275309168,
                "title": "The Price of Prisons: What Incarceration Costs Taxpayers",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "the-price-of-prisons-what-incarceration-costs-taxpayers-28jn5bstnb",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "Federal Sentencing Reporter",
                    "official_page": "http://fsr.ucpress.edu/",
                    "issn": "1053-9867",
                    "alias": [
                        "FSR"
                    ],
                    "id": 132085067,
                    "slug": "federal-sentencing-reporter",
                    "unique_id": "141b00sp",
                    "full_slug": "federal-sentencing-reporter-141b00sp"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2012-10-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1525/FSR.2012.25.1.68",
                "unique_id": "28jn5bstnb",
                "abstract": "Persistent fiscal challenges in the United States have spurred greater scrutiny of government spending. States' corrections expenditures, which have nearly quadrupled over the past two decades, are receiving considerable attention. These circumstances make it crucial for policy makers and the public to understand the full cost of prisons to taxpayers\u2014something that is easier said than done. Although corrections departments pay the vast majority of costs for state prisons, other departments pay related expenses\u2014some of which are substantial. Depending on the state, these can include employee benefits, capital costs, in-prison education services, or hospital care for inmates. Additionally, the cost of underfunded contributions for corrections employees' pension and retiree health care plans must be included in a comprehensive accounting of prison costs. In partnership with the Pew Center on the States, staff from the Vera Institute of Justice's Center on Sentencing and Corrections and Cost-Benefit Analysis Unit developed a methodology for calculating the full cost of prisons to taxpayers. The application of this methodology, which was developed in collaboration with a panel of advisers in the fields of corrections and public finance and field-tested in five states, is the subject of this report. Vera researchers found that the total taxpayer cost of prisons in the 40 states that participated in this study was 13.9 percent higher than the cost reflected in those states' combined corrections budgets. The total price to taxpayers was $39 billion, $5.4 billion more than the $33.5 billion reflected in corrections budgets alone. The greatest cost drivers outside corrections departments were as follows: > underfunded contributions to retiree health care for corrections employees ($1.9 billion); > states' contributions to retiree health care on behalf of their corrections departments ($837 million); > employee benefits, such as health insurance ($613 million); > states' contributions to pensions on behalf of their corrections departments ($598 million); > capital costs ($485 million); > hospital and other health care for the prison population ($335 million); and > underfunded pension contributions for corrections employees ($304 million). Among the participating states, costs outside the corrections department ranged from less than 1 percent of the total cost of prisons, in Arizona, to as much as 34 percent in Connecticut. The extra costs accounted for less than 5 percent of total prison costs in 16 states, 5 to 9.9 percent of total prison costs in nine states, and 10 to 19.9 percent of total prison costs in nine \u2026",
