            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 55/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses colonial prison labor value to assess economic impact; details food and staff costs; lacks modern state/federal budget data and demographic analysis; treats forced labor as revenue, but not broader revenue mechanisms.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "This paper studies the role of labor coercion in fiscal capacity building in Nigeria during the colonial era. We estimate the value gained from labor coercion and compare this to other colonial expenditure using evidence from British colonial Nigeria. We use datasets on wages and prisoners from 1920 through 1938 to examine the evolution of value gained from labor coercion during the period. Our results show that coerced convict labor made up a significant part of colonial expenditures. The results provide new insights on the costs of colonial infrastructure and the effects of the colonial revenue imperative on African populations.",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "The value of labor coercion from convict labor was significant, peaking at over \u00a3236,000 in 1926. This value was often larger than overall prison expenditure, exceeding it in 5 out of 9 years between 1920 and 1930. It also represented a significant share of public works expenditure, ranging between almost 40% and 140% at its peak. Food costs were the largest expenditure category for prisoners, accounting for 59% in 1920, followed by salaries for prison officials at 19%.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "The paper notes that in Nigeria's Southern Provinces, between 73% and 91% of prisoners engaged in hard or light labor from 1920 to 1937, with over 70% in hard labor. Most prisoners in provincial or divisional prisons were unskilled laborers with short sentences (less than 2 years), primarily for tax defaults and petty thefts. An example from Owerri prison in South-Eastern Nigeria shows a sizable number of inmates were young men who resisted mandated labor. No further details on age distribution, elderly populations, healthcare needs by age, or specific demographic characteristics like gender or socioeconomic status are provided.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "In 1920, the largest expenditure category on prisoners was food or 'rations' (59%), followed by salaries of prison officials (19%) (p. 24). In 1925, the average food cost of a prisoner per day in the Southern Province was 6.26 pence (p. 25). Costs in Southern provinces were sometimes more than 4 times higher than in Northern provinces (p. 25). The value of labor coercion from prison labor was equivalent to more than 3% of overall colonial expenditure at its peak (p. 23).",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "In 1920, food (rations) constituted 59% of prison expenditure, followed by salaries for prison officials at 19%. For all available years, food and official salaries were the top two spending categories. Annual average food costs per prisoner in 1925 were 6.26 pence per day in the Southern Province. This is significantly higher than alternative food price baskets (cassava at 0.41 pence/pound, maize at 1.63 pence/pound). The value of labor coercion (unpaid wages) peaked at over \u00a3236,000 in 1926. This value was larger than overall prison expenditure for 5 out of 9 years between 1920-1930 and exceeded public works expenditure in 5 out of 9 years pre-1930.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "The paper discusses the use of convict labor in the United States during the 18th and 19th centuries, noting that public works projects like roads and railroads were sometimes entirely funded by convict labor (Abrams, 1976). However, it does not provide specific economic data such as per-prisoner costs, total system costs, state-by-state variations, medical costs, food costs, revenue generation, or demographic cost factors for US incarceration. The focus is primarily on British colonial Nigeria.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "The paper mentions the use of convict labor in the United States during the 18th and 19th centuries, noting a state-led effort to criminalize Black populations following the abolition of slavery in 1865. It highlights the use of primarily Black prison labor for public works projects like roads and railroads in the US South. The text also notes that laws such as vagrancy laws and Black Codes sometimes increased the prison population during periods of higher labor demand and shortages. No specific percentages, age, gender, or elderly prisoner statistics for the US are provided."
        },
