            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 55/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses cost per inmate, construction spending, and aging\u2011prisoner health expenses to illustrate economic impacts; provides aggregate budget figures but lacks detailed cost component breakdowns and revenue analysis.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "The total bill for incarcerating the nation's two million inmates was estimated to reach $40 billion in 2000. Annually, it costs approximately $20,000 to confine a state inmate and $24,000 for a federal inmate. Florida spent $230 million on prisoner health care in one year, roughly one-fifth of its prison budget. Health care costs for prisoners over fifty are three times higher than for younger inmates. Between 1987 and 1998, corrections spending increased 30%, while higher education budgets decreased by 18.2%.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "The U.S. prison population is projected to reach two million inmates in 2001, with 1.3 million nonviolent offenders. Mandatory drug penalties have disproportionately impacted nonwhite offenders, with African-Americans comprising 35% of drug arrests, 55% of convictions, and 74% of sentences, despite 76% of drug users being white. The overabundance of long-term mandatory minimums will create an older prison population, increasing healthcare costs, as costs for prisoners over fifty are three times higher than for younger inmates.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "- Total bill for incarcerating prisoners at state and federal levels was estimated at $41 billion for the year 2000.\n- Approximately $26 billion was spent incarcerating 1.3 million nonviolent offenders in 2000.\n- Average annual cost to confine a state inmate is $20,000, and a federal inmate is $24,000.\n- Total costs for constructing state prisons in 1997 were $3.4 billion.\n- Florida spent $230 million on prisoner health care last year, roughly one-fifth of its prison budget.\n- Health care costs for Florida prisoners over fifty are three times that of younger inmates.\n- Between 1987 and 1998, corrections spending increased 30%, while higher education budgets decreased 18.2%.",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "-   Annual incarceration costs are approximately $20,000 for a state inmate and $24,000 for a federal inmate.\n-   The total bill for incarcerating 2 million inmates was expected to reach $40 billion in 2000, including $26 billion for 1.3 million nonviolent offenders.\n-   Florida spent $230 million on prisoner health care last year, roughly one-fifth of its prison budget.\n-   Health care costs for Florida prisoners over fifty are estimated to be three times that of a younger inmate.\n-   The average cost of incarcerating an elderly (55+) state inmate is estimated at $69,000 annually.\n-   Construction of state prisons in 1997 cost $3.4 billion, financed through long-term bonds.\n-   Between 1990 and 1995, 168 state and 45 federal prisons were constructed.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "The U.S. prison population was estimated to top two million inmates in 2001, with a total cost of $41 billion in 2000, including $26 billion for 1.3 million nonviolent offenders. Annual incarceration costs average $20,000 for a state inmate and $24,000 for a federal inmate. Florida spent $230 million on prisoner health care in one year, roughly one-fifth of its prison budget. Health care costs for prisoners over fifty are three times higher than for younger inmates.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "The U.S. prison population reached over two million inmates by 2001, a five-fold increase since 1972, with incarceration rates six to ten times higher than other industrial nations. This includes 1.3 million nonviolent offenders. Over 88% of those arrested for crack possession are African-American, and while 76% of drug users are white, African-Americans comprise 35% of drug arrests, 55% of convictions, and 74% of drug sentences. An aging prison population is emerging, with health care costs for prisoners over 50 estimated at three times that of younger inmates."
        },
