            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 55/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses Michigan per\u2011prisoner cost data to examine economic impact; cites medical parole and officer wages, but lacks detailed expense categories, budget statements, and revenue mechanisms.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "Michigan's per-prisoner medical costs were about 108% of the national average, spending $2,841 per prisoner in 2001, compared to a national average of $4,370 per person. The state spent approximately $300 million annually on medical care, receiving poor value. Michigan's per-prisoner incarceration cost averaged $28,743, exceeding the federal average of $23,429 and state average of $23,876. Wages and benefits constituted 71% of the total operating costs of the Michigan prison system.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "Michigan's prison population includes a high number of lifers and long sentences for drug crimes, leading to many older prisoners needing more expensive medical care. The average sentence length for lifers gaining release increased to 23.2 years (2000-2004), with many released on medical parole due to terminal illness or incapacitating medical/mental health conditions. The paper does not provide specific age distribution, racial, gender, or socioeconomic status demographics of the general inmate population, nor does it detail treatment differences by age, beyond the general mention of older prisoners having greater healthcare needs.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "- Michigan's per-prisoner medical costs were about 108% of the national average in 2001.\n- Michigan spent $2,841 per prisoner on medical care in 2001, compared to a national average of $4,370 per person.\n- By 2003, the nation spent $61 billion on corrections, out of $186 billion total criminal justice spending.\n- The average cost of incarceration per prisoner was $22,650.\n- Michigan's average per-prisoner cost was $28,743, exceeding the federal average ($23,429) and state average ($23,876).\n- Wages and benefits made up 71% of Michigan's total prison system operating costs.",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "Michigan's per-prisoner medical costs were 108% of the national average. In 2001, Michigan spent $2,841 per prisoner on medical care, compared to a national average of $4,370 per person. By 2003, the nation spent $61 billion on corrections, out of a $186 billion criminal justice system budget. The average incarceration cost per prisoner was $22,650. Michigan's average per-prisoner cost was $28,743, exceeding the federal average ($23,429) and state average ($23,876). Wages and benefits comprised 71% of Michigan's total prison operating costs, typically two-thirds nationally. Correctional officer base pay in Michigan ranked sixth highest in 2002.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "In 2003, the U.S. spent $61 billion on corrections out of $186 billion total for the criminal justice system. The average per-prisoner cost was $22,650. Michigan's per-prisoner cost was $28,743, exceeding the national average. Michigan's per-prisoner medical costs in 2001 were $2,841, about 108% of the national average. Wages and benefits constituted 71% of Michigan's prison system operating costs. Older prisoners and those with serious medical needs tend to increase costs.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "The paper indicates that Michigan has the sixth-largest prison population among U.S. states, and ranks eighth in total population. It notes that three states with larger populations (Ohio, Illinois, Pennsylvania) have smaller prison populations. Michigan's high incarceration rate is not solely due to crime rates, as it ranks 11th in incarceration rate but 17th in crime rates. Over 9% of Michigan's prison population (nearly 4,600 prisoners) served life sentences in 2002-2003, making it the fourth-largest number of lifers nationally. Many released lifers had terminal illnesses or incapacitating medical conditions."
        },
