            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 55/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses elderly inmate health expenses to illustrate rising incarceration costs; details expensive medical technologies and their budget impact; links age demographics directly to cost spikes; lacks broader cost breakdown, budget tables, or revenue mechanisms.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "Massachusetts has very high state prison costs, with an average of $45,000 per prisoner per year in 2013, estimated at over $50,000 annually. Incarcerating elderly prisoners (50 and over) costs two to four times more than the overall average. The LWOP subpopulation alone requires over $80 million annually. A 22-bed chronic care unit is estimated at $3.5 million annually, or $160,000 per prisoner. Releasing an elderly prisoner on parole could save $66,294 to over $100,000 annually.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "Massachusetts' prison population has significantly aged, with those 50 and older comprising 24% of state prisoners by January 2015, up from 19.4% in 2011. This increase is driven by longer sentences, not rising elderly crime. Elderly prisoners (50+) are considered physiologically 10-15 years older, often having premature aging due to confinement stress, substandard healthcare, and mental illness. They are much less likely to recidivate. Healthcare costs for elderly prisoners are two to four times higher than the average. The paper does not address race, gender, or socioeconomic status of the prison population.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "Massachusetts state prison costs averaged $45,000 per prisoner per year in 2013, estimated at over $50,000 annually. Costs for elderly prisoners (50 and over) are two to four times greater than the overall average. In North Carolina, 72% of healthcare costs were for prisoners aged 50 and older. A projected 22-bed chronic care unit at Lemuel Shattuck Hospital would cost $3.5 million annually ($160,000 per prisoner).",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "Massachusetts state prison costs averaged $45,000 per prisoner per year in 2013, estimated over $50,000 annually currently. Elderly prisoners (50 and over) cost 2-4 times the overall average. In Florida, 50+ prisoners (11% of population) accounted for 38% of medical costs; in North Carolina, 72% of healthcare costs were for 50+ prisoners. The Lemuel Shattuck Hospital proposed a 22-bed unit for 22 prisoners at $160,000 per prisoner annually. MPCH estimated personnel costs for a 90-bed facility at $7.5 million/year. DOC allocates less than 2% of budget to programs ($10.8M in FY2011, $10.7M in FY2013). Staffing accounts for almost 70% of DOC budgets.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "Massachusetts state prison costs were $45,000 per prisoner per year in 2013, estimated over $50,000 annually. Incarcerating elderly prisoners (50 and over) costs two to four times the average. In Florida, prisoners aged 50+ (11% of population) accounted for 38% of medical costs; in North Carolina, 72% of healthcare costs. Releasing an elderly prisoner could save $94,578-$144,578 annually in Massachusetts. Correctional costs increased by 13.9% on average due to hidden collateral expenses in 40 states.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "The Massachusetts prison population is among the most elderly in the nation, with 24% of state prisoners aged 50 and over by January 2015, compared to a national average of 16.7%. LWOP prisoners represent 11% of the total prison population in Massachusetts. Overall, the elderly prisoner population is increasing, while younger age groups are decreasing. Specific race/ethnicity breakdowns, gender ratios, or broader geographic concentrations for the U.S. are not detailed."
        },
        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "id": 2346375085,
                "title": "Aging American Prisons: Length of stay, dying behind bars, and implications for public health",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "aging-american-prisons-length-of-stay-dying-behind-bars-and-odl207vbmi",
                "journal": null,
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2016-01-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": null,
                "doi": null,
                "unique_id": "odl207vbmi",
                "abstract": "One notable consequence of mass incarceration is the growing population of elderly prison inmates in the U.S. This growth raises questions concerning the causes and implications of such a change, as housing older prisoners places a financial strain on state and federal correctional systems. Because this added cost is largely a result of medical needs, the growing elderly inmate population has raised questions about the impact of incarceration on health. This study investigates the causes of this \u201cgraying\u201d of American prisons and its potential effects on correctional and community health. Using National Corrections Reporting Program data from 1990 to 2009, I compare changes in offenders\u2019 age at release (mean age & proportion 55 and older), age at admission, average length of stay, and the proportion of \u201creleased\u201d inmates who died in prison as an indicator of correctional health. I find that while each of these measures increased over time, length of stay can explain only a small portion of the increase in release age; most of which is due to an increase in age at admission. Further, while the increase in offender age can account, fully, for the increase in prison deaths over time, length-of-stay is also among the strongest predictors of death in prison, even controlling for age. The results suggests that the incarceration of a growing number of elderly prisoners, coupled with the harmful effects of lengthy prison stays, may have a notable impact on health both within correctional systems and in the communities to which most will return. AGING AMERICAN PRISONS 4 TABLE OF CONTENTS Acknowledgements 2 Abstract 3 Introduction 5 What We Know 8 Current Objectives 18 Data and Methods 193 Introduction 5 What We Know 8 Current Objectives 18 Data and Methods 19 Results 21 Discussion 25 Conclusion 28 AGING AMERICAN PRISONS 5",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 1,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Stephanie G Barger",
                            "id": 2510994484,
                            "slug": "stephanie-g-barger",
