            },
            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 52/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses aging inmate data to quantify medical and housing costs, links age demographics to per\u2011prisoner expenses, but lacks detailed budget tables and revenue analysis.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "One notable consequence of mass incarceration is the growing population of elderly prison inmates in the U.S. This growth raises questions concerning the causes and implications of such a change, as housing older prisoners places a financial strain on state and federal correctional systems. Because this added cost is largely a result of medical needs, the growing elderly inmate population has raised questions about the impact of incarceration on health. This study investigates the causes of this \u201cgraying\u201d of American prisons and its potential effects on correctional and community health. Using National Corrections Reporting Program data from 1990 to 2009, I compare changes in offenders\u2019 age at release (mean age & proportion 55 and older), age at admission, average length of stay, and the proportion of \u201creleased\u201d inmates who died in prison as an indicator of correctional health. I find that while each of these measures increased over time, length of stay can explain only a small portion of the increase in release age; most of which is due to an increase in age at admission. Further, while the increase in offender age can account, fully, for the increase in prison deaths over time, length-of-stay is also among the strongest predictors of death in prison, even controlling for age. The results suggests that the incarceration of a growing number of elderly prisoners, coupled with the harmful effects of lengthy prison stays, may have a notable impact on health both within correctional systems and in the communities to which most will return. AGING AMERICAN PRISONS 4 TABLE OF CONTENTS Acknowledgements 2 Abstract 3 Introduction 5 What We Know 8 Current Objectives 18 Data and Methods 193 Introduction 5 What We Know 8 Current Objectives 18 Data and Methods 19 Results 21 Discussion 25 Conclusion 28 AGING AMERICAN PRISONS 5",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "Housing older inmates is more expensive than younger ones due to increased medical needs. The American Civil Liberties Union estimated that incarcerating an older inmate costs two to three times more than the average inmate, with an annual cost of $68,270 for those aged 50 or older, compared to $34,135 for the average inmate. State and federal corrections cost American taxpayers around $77 billion annually, with 10-20 percent of prison operating budgets allocated to healthcare, which is the fastest-growing portion. States could save an average of $66,294 annually per released aging prisoner.",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "The prison population age 55 and older increased by 444 percent between 1991 and 2014, significantly faster than the general prison population. Elderly inmates (defined as 55+) have an average length-of-stay of 3.75 years and account for 6.3 percent of deaths in prison. Inmates age 45 or older reported health problems at 67.5 percent, compared to 24.2 percent for those under 25. The sample was 90 percent male, with 36.7 percent white, 44.3 percent black, and 15.4 percent Hispanic.",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "Incarceration for an older inmate costs two to three times more than for the average inmate. The average annual cost per prisoner is $34,135, while for an inmate age 50 or older, it is $68,270 (Chettiear, Bunting, & Schotter, 2012). State and federal corrections cost around $77 billion annually, with 10-20 percent of prison operating budgets devoted to healthcare, which is the fastest-growing portion (Auerhahn, 2002). States could save an average of $66,294 annually per released aging prisoner (2012 ACLU report).",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "Incarceration for an older inmate costs two to three times more than the average inmate. The average annual cost per prisoner is $34,135, while for an inmate age 50 or older, it is $68,270. Annually, state and federal corrections cost American taxpayers around $77 billion, with roughly 90 percent allocated to incarceration. Of prison operating budgets, 10-20 percent is devoted to healthcare, which is the fastest growing portion. States could save an average of $66,294 annually per released aging prisoner. No specific data on daily costs, food costs, staffing costs, infrastructure costs, or revenue generation is provided.",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "In the U.S., housing an older inmate costs two to three times more than an average inmate. The average annual cost per prisoner is $34,135, while for an inmate age 50 or older, it's $68,270. Annually, state and federal corrections cost taxpayers around $77 billion, with 90% allocated to incarceration. Healthcare accounts for 10-20% of prison operating budgets and is the fastest-growing cost. States could save an average of $66,294 annually per released aging prisoner.",
            "demographics_igYX7G": "Between 1990 and 2009, the sample of 7,000,233 inmates was 90% male. Racially, 36.7% were White, 44.3% Black, and 15.4% Hispanic. The average admission age was 31.8 years, and average release age was 33.8 years. The prison population age 55 and older increased by 444 percent between 1991 and 2014, while the proportion of all prison inmates over age 54 grew by almost 200 percent. Data from 33 states were included in this study."
        },
