                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": null,
                "doi": null,
                "unique_id": "58stzxl2ia",
                "abstract": "Jails\u2014locally run facilities used primarily to detain persons arrested but not yet convicted of any crime\u2014now hold more than 730,000 people on any given day, more than triple their population in 1983. These are the places where most arrested men and women land and where many remain as their cases wind through the criminal justice system. Bigger jail populations mean increased costs for staff and other expenses. The U.S. Department of Justice estimated that local communities spent $22.2 billion on jails in 2011.",
                "authors": {
