        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "unique_id": "2dag68w408",
                "id": 2282958186,
                "full_slug": "cost-and-punishment-reassessing-incarceration-costs-and-the-2dag68w408",
                "title": "Cost and punishment: Reassessing incarceration costs and the value of college-in-prison programs",
                "abstract": "This article argues that college-in-prison programs are an effective response to prison population growth and cost explosions - admittedly on a limited scale. The programs reduce long-term costs through investments in education. Such offerings are not suitable for every prisoner, but can be highly effective for those individuals in a position to benefit from devoting time in prison to learning. The article begins with an overview of the untenable situation in U.S. prisons, including the burden of the population and cost boom in prisons across the country. Second, the article examines the prison population to determine which individuals there might benefit from education. Third, the study considers theories of penology and the place an education program might occupy in the respective theories. Fourth, the article describes college-in-prison programs and their efforts to address the needs of both prisoners and the populace paying for the prison system. Finally, the article considers the role of college-in-prison programs as part of the risk management paradigm currently predominant in criminal justice systems.",
