        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "id": 2084657744,
                "title": "The Corrections Perspective",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "the-corrections-perspective-4yffxjsxj4",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "Journal of Correctional Health Care",
                    "official_page": "http://jcx.sagepub.com/",
                    "issn": "1078-3458",
                    "alias": [
                        "JCHC"
                    ],
                    "id": 117112776,
                    "slug": "journal-of-correctional-health-care",
                    "unique_id": "1ce8cyau",
                    "full_slug": "journal-of-correctional-health-care-1ce8cyau"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2004-10-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1177/107834580401000407",
                "unique_id": "4yffxjsxj4",
                "abstract": "In 1993, a federal district judge closed out the 21 -year-old lawsuit that had placed health care in the Florida Department of Corrections (FDOC) under a court injunction and monitor. The court noted that the FDOC had met a minimal constitutional standard for the care of its inmates. To achieve this status the FDOC system was spending almost 19% of its total budget on medical services. These services included a broad array of physical care, mental health care, pharmacy, nursing operations, and dental services, all housed within the FDOC's Office of Health Services (OHS). In 1995, under new direction for clinical services, OHS decided to adopt a health care delivery system in which it would do everything a medical school did except be accredited for teaching. Among the reasons this approach was taken was the realization that it would be more cost-effective. OHS also realized that the implementation of this type of health care delivery within a large correctional bureaucracy would be novel and possibly difficult.",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 2,
                    "data": [
