            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 10/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Not Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses health\u2011service budget data (19% of total) to illustrate medical cost share, but lacks broader economic analysis, budget-wide breakdowns, demographic cost links, or revenue discussion.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "In 1993, a federal district judge closed out the 21 -year-old lawsuit that had placed health care in the Florida Department of Corrections (FDOC) under a court injunction and monitor. The court noted that the FDOC had met a minimal constitutional standard for the care of its inmates. To achieve this status the FDOC system was spending almost 19% of its total budget on medical services. These services included a broad array of physical care, mental health care, pharmacy, nursing operations, and dental services, all housed within the FDOC's Office of Health Services (OHS). In 1995, under new direction for clinical services, OHS decided to adopt a health care delivery system in which it would do everything a medical school did except be accredited for teaching. Among the reasons this approach was taken was the realization that it would be more cost-effective. OHS also realized that the implementation of this type of health care delivery within a large correctional bureaucracy would be novel and possibly difficult.",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "",
            "specific-cost_RNUson": "",
            "key-economic_rSp2Jg": "",
            "demographics_igYX7G": ""
        },
        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "id": 2414024229,
                "title": "Prison health care.",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "prison-health-care-4ps0wnuokm",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "South African Medical Journal",
                    "official_page": "http://www.samj.org.za/",
                    "issn": "0256-9574",
                    "alias": [
                        "SAMJ"
                    ],
                    "id": 147026946,
                    "slug": "south-african-medical-journal",
                    "unique_id": "1rkf0209",
                    "full_slug": "south-african-medical-journal-1rkf0209"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "1996-06-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": null,
                "unique_id": "4ps0wnuokm",
                "abstract": "A high percentage of the more than 2 million inmates in U.S. jails and prisons suffer from mental illness, addiction or infectious and chronic diseases like HIV/AIDS and diabetes. About a quarter suffer from major depression and a fifth from psychosis. Many had little or no health care before being incarcerated. Providing treatment and preventive care for prisoners who eventually return to society can help stem the spread of infectious disease in communities and keep those with mental illness and addiction from landing back in jail, say public-health officials. While prisoners are, ironically, the only Americans who have a constitutionally guaranteed right to health care, most prison health systems are underfunded and understaffed, making the care they provide spotty at best. Meanwhile, strict sentencing guidelines and three-strikes-andyou\u2019re-out laws have created a burgeoning \u2014 and aging \u2014 prisoner population, which is driving skyrocketing health-care costs even higher. I N S I D E THE ISSUES............................3 BACKGROUND ....................10 CHRONOLOGY ....................11 AT ISSUE ............................17 CURRENT SITUATION ............18 OUTLOOK ..........................19 BIBLIOGRAPHY ....................22 THE NEXT STEP ..................23 THISREPORT Bobby Sutherland is among 300 inmates at an Alabama prison for the aged and infirm. He is serving 297 years on rape and pornography charges and expects to die behind bars. CQResearcher Published by CQ Press, a division of Congressional Quarterly Inc. www.cqresearcher.com",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 2,
