            "relevance_ZwZUXa": "- **Relevance Score:** 7/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Not Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses judicial decision analysis to discuss how courts influence correctional spending, but lacks detailed cost data, expense breakdowns, demographic cost links, or revenue mechanisms.",
            "abstract_eTrloI": "Few people would deny that the courts make public policy. But while this fact may be acknowledged, not everyone is pleased with it. Several years ago, critics of the so-called \"imperial judiciary\" viewed judicial intervention in mental health, prison reform, educational finance, and school desegregation as encroachments on legislative prerogatives.' Presidents Nixon and Reagan have ensured that their judicial appointees have held a more modest image of judicial activism. Moreover, various constitutional proposals on school prayer and abortion are designed, in part, to limit further the policy-making ability of the courts. Still, even if there is some retreat from the imperial judiciary, we can be reasonably sure that the federal courts will continue to influence public policy. When they do, the burden of implementation will continue to fall on state governments.",
            "key-financial_HGDnyX": "",
            "demographics_sGfiV7": "",
            "cost-data-and_I7WOQt": "",
