        {
            "papers_j8QP6r": {
                "id": 4042799091,
                "title": "Ageing in prison",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "ageing-in-prison-1kvljpo230",
                "journal": null,
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2023-12-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": null,
                "publication_type": "Book Chapter",
                "doi": "10.1093/med/9780198834533.003.0013",
                "unique_id": "1kvljpo230",
                "abstract": "Abstract This chapter begins with an introduction to ageing in prison. In recent years there has been a dramatic rise in the number of older people in prison in the UK, partly owing to harsher sentencing, the increased willingness of the courts to imprison older people, and an increase of long life of those in the prison system. However, the corollary of this is that the effects of ageing are magnified in the prison setting as the people have experienced a lifetime of health and socioeconomic disadvantage. The chapter explains the health and social care of the ageing prison population, including standard geriatric syndromes, polypharmacy, palliative and end-of-line care, and access to specialist services. Adaptations, buddy schemes, and voluntary sector input are discussed. The chapter goes on to cover specific issues, such as dementia, osteoporosis, and compassionate release. ",
                "is_fulltext_request_allowed": false,
                "metrics": {
                    "citations": {
                        "total": null
                    }
                },
                "tldr": "Ageing in prison involves magnified effects of ageing due to health and socioeconomic disadvantage. The chapter covers health and social care, including geriatric syndromes, polypharmacy, palliative and end-of-line care, and access to specialist services.",
                "podcast": null,
